<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Washington Naval Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Washington Naval Treaty was an arms-limitation agreement signed in February 1922 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-naval-conference">Washington Naval Conference</a>. It fixed ratios for the tonnage of capital ships, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the United States parity, halted battleship building for ten years, and lapsed after Japan withdrew in the 1930s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/washington-naval-treaty.jpg" alt="Washington Naval Treaty" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washington_Naval_Treaty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the First World War Britain still had the world's largest navy, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and, more distantly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. With the German threat seemingly over, a naval arms race among the allies seemed likely. American plans of 1916 to 1919 called for 50 modern battleships, and in 1920 the Japanese Diet authorised an eight-eight programme of eight battleships and eight battlecruisers. The American public and Congress disliked the new race, and Britain could ill afford the cost of capital ships. In November 1921 the administration of Warren Harding called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-naval-conference">Washington Naval Conference</a>, partly to forestall a British plan for a conference on the Pacific and Far East.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>At the opening session on 21 November 1921 Secretary of State Charles Evans Hughes declared that the way to disarm is to disarm, and proposed a ten-year pause in capital ship construction and the scrapping of existing or planned ships to a ratio of 5:5:3 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The treaty, finalised on 6 February 1922, set tonnage ratios of approximately 5:5:3:1.75:1.75 for the United Kingdom, the United States, Japan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Capital ships were limited to 35,000 tons and 16-inch guns, aircraft carriers to 27,000 tons, and all other warships to 10,000 tons and 8-inch guns. The United States had to scrap 30 existing or planned capital ships, Britain 23 and Japan 17.</p><p>Article XIX barred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from building new fortifications or naval bases in the Pacific, though existing ones at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> could stay. That provision helped win Japanese acceptance of the limits. Attempts to cap cruisers and submarines failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty ended a long period of rising battleship construction, and many ships under construction were scrapped or converted into aircraft carriers. The London Naval Treaty of 1930 extended its limits, but the Second London Naval Treaty of 1936 was largely ineffective without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The American delegates had also made it clear that they would not agree unless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> ended its alliance with Japan, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-conference">Imperial Conference</a> of 1921 had already decided not to renew.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the treaty split the navy between the Treaty Faction and the Fleet Faction. The delegation's director, Katō Tomosaburō, accepted the lower ratio rather than an arms race the country would lose, while Katō Kanji opposed it. Isoroku Yamamoto argued for remaining in the treaty. On 29 December 1934 Japan gave formal notice of termination, and the provisions stayed in force until the end of 1936. American cryptographers had read Japanese delegation communications during the conference.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Washington Naval Treaty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
